<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

  <!--
    OWASP dependency-check suppressions.

    A suppression is an assertion that a reported CVE does not apply to THIS application.
    It is a security judgement, not a way to quiet the build, so every entry must record
    who decided and why. An undocumented suppression is worse than the finding: it hides
    a real risk behind the appearance of a clean report.

    Only suppress after triage establishes one of:
      1. FALSE POSITIVE  - dependency-check matched the wrong product or version.
                           Check "CPE Confidence" in the report; LOW/MEDIUM is suspect.
      2. NOT REACHABLE   - the vulnerable code path cannot be reached in this app, e.g.
                           the feature is disabled, or the input is never attacker
                           controlled. State the assumption, because assumptions expire.
      3. NO FIX + ACCEPTED - no patched version exists and the risk is consciously taken.
                           Prefer <until> so it comes back for review.

    If a fixed version exists, upgrade instead. Do not suppress.

    Regenerate the report with:  ./mvnw dependency-check:check -Dformat=ALL
    The CSV output is the practical triage worklist.

    ===========================================================================
    2026-11-03: the first scan (dependency-check 13.0.0, Boot 4.1.2, Spring AI 2.0.2)
    reported no findings at all, so there is nothing to suppress. The entries
    message-inspector carries (log4j-api, swagger-ui, jackson-databind 2.x) are not
    copied: none of their findings was reported here. Add an entry only after
    triaging a finding this scan actually reports.
    ===========================================================================
  -->

</suppressions>
